  "body": "**No new \u03ba.** Main's cover numbers (`research/format-limits` \u00a7D, #595) establish that the 63 readers of a bit source (E7 = t\u22a5) need three hyperplane sections: two flats cover at most 52 lines, and some three D6 flats cover all 63. This package classifies the covers exactly.\n\n## Change and mathematical scope\n\nOne self-contained package, `research/e7-flat-cover-classification/`, plus one path-filtered workflow and one `.gitattributes` line. No existing file changes.\n\n1. **The covering triples are exactly the D4 frames through t.**\n   - Three hyperplane flats cover E7 if and only if they are E7 \u2229 a\u22a5, E7 \u2229 b\u22a5, E7 \u2229 c\u22a5 for an orthogonal frame {t, a, b, c} spanning a D4.\n   - There are 315 such triples, one per D4 through t.\n   - The other 3780 orthogonal frames span only 4A1, and their triples miss at least 8 lines.\n2. **The near-covers are exactly the A3 subsystems through t.** A near-cover is a triple covering exactly 62 of the 63 lines.\n   - Each near-cover is E6(A) + E6(B) + D6(m). Here A and B are the two A2s through t of the A3, and m is the A3's line orthogonal to t, which is the missed line.\n   - There are 378 = 63 \u00d7 6, and no other type occurs.\n3. **The cover count does not force 3 registers per D6 pool.**\n   - In a covering triple, each D6 has 16 private lines, which span it.\n   - Two rank-5 subflats never cover a whole D6 (at most 28 of 30). But they do cover its 16 private lines (75 pairs per D6), and six rank-5 flats, two per D6, cover all of E7.\n   - So \"two 5-dim subflats cover \u2264 28 of 30\" does not alone give the proposed 180 \u00d7 3 = 540 count.\n\n**Consequence for bit skeletons.**\n- The D4 skeletons of #493, #510 and #543 read each target through the D6 triple of its 4-frame. By (1), every three-section top layer has this form.\n- The only near-alternative is (2), with one line read separately.\n\n**Scope.**\n- These are statements about covers by flats, the model of \u00a7D. They do not price a skeleton.\n- (3) does not construct a 2-per-pool design: frame nesting, the readers' frames and restoration are not modelled.\n\n**Duplicates.**\n- Main has the flat census, the best pair and the existence of a D6 cover. It does not classify covers or near-covers.\n- #463 has the two-section carrier bound.\n- Checked against main at `f567884a` and against the titles and bodies of every hub PR through #883.\n\n## Validation\n\n`python3 -B research/e7-flat-cover-classification/cover_check.py` uses the standard library only.\n\n- **Run.** It prints `PASS_E7_FLAT_COVER_CLASSIFICATION (21 checks)` in about 6 minutes. It passed on Linux aarch64 with Python 3.12.3 and 3.11.16, against main at `f567884a`. CI runs Python 3.11 and 3.14.\n- **Exact arithmetic.** Ranks are computed mod 2\u00b3\u00b9 \u2212 1. By Hadamard, minors of at most 8 norm-4 rows are at most 256 in absolute value, so these equal the rational ranks.\n- **Exhaustive searches.** Every search is exhaustive.\n- **Agreement with main.** The checker confirms main's pinned D1 values (8821 flats, the size histogram, the best pair 52, cover number 3) both from its own census and from the text of `d1_cover_numbers.py`.\n\n## Credits and AI disclosure\n\n- DreamingOfClouds: #595's cover-number census and #463's carrier bound.\n- eumemic: the D4 skeleton of #493 and #543.\n- Prepared by DaysSky with assistance from Claude.\n",
